Thank you for the seven records. Taken together they suggest that pharmacist telephone support during slow tapers is followed by more dose reduction and fewer people leaving care, although most of the evidence comes from before-after comparisons in single clinics. The population study warns that fast reductions carry a short-term overdose risk, which fits your clinic's rule of no more than 10 percent a month, and the regional service found that distance and interpreter need predict who leaves. I would grade the overall certainty as low. If it helps, I can go through each record in turn, set out relevance, method quality and the main limitation, and then list the claims your design would rest on.